A||||7||Huck||2.8 suddenly only firing on 4 cyl? whats wrong||||5||
Z||000000||Huck||11-04-2001||03:48 PM||||I came home this weekend to visit my parents, and as soon as I got to their town, I went around a corner and it stalled on me.  I started my baby back up no problem, but it was running very very weak.  I managed to limp to my fathers house with it, and checked it the next morning.  <P>It smelled like a snowmobile, very rich in fuel.  I pulled some wires off my distributor, and sure enough 2 different wires I pulled off didn't make it stumble or change the way it ran at all.  They ran to the 2 plugs side by side at the back, passenger side of the engine.  I thought ok, plugs or wires...  so I changed my plugs and wires, but there was no difference at all in the way that it ran.  My mechanical background is quite limited, so now I am stumped as to what to try next.  The biggest problem, is that my fiero is my only car right now, and I need to get home tonight so I can go to work tomorrow... its a 2 hour drive.<P>ANybody have any suggestions as to what may be wrong, or more things I can check?<P>thanks<BR>Huck<BR>87 GT T-tops<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||Mach10||11-04-2001||04:16 PM||||frazzled distributer cap/rotor, or else the injectors are stuck open and hosing the cylinder down with fuel, making it too rich to reliably run? Pop the dist. cap off, and make sure the points are messed... <BR>The only thing else I can think of, sine they are next to each other, is that you blew the head gasket, and it's not building any compression in those cylinders. Does the exhaust smell sweet, as well as rich? Is it puffing white smoke? Beware of exceptionally clean plugs; steam cleaned... Check oil for water contamination. Try a compression test... rule out the expensive before replacing anything else.<P><p>[This message has been edited by Mach10 (edited 11-04-2001).]||||reg||3
Z||000002||Huck||11-04-2001||04:28 PM||||Thanks a lot for the quick reply.<BR>It is not puffing white smoke, the exhaust looks completely normal.  All fluids are up, but my oil pressure is higher than it normally is, nearly at the 500 mark.  Im gonna hunt down a compression tester right now, and test those 2 cylinders...  I really hope its not going to be too serious, though I am starting to accept the fact that I am going to have to get a ride / take a bus back to London yet tonight.<P>I guess I could also take the distributor cap etc off and clean it up, though I have lots of spark at all the contact points.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||rogergarrison||11-04-2001||05:34 PM||||I wouldnt think that just 2 contacts in distrib went bad. Since its 2 ajoining cylinders, Id be more suspect of head or gasket. your comp chk should let you know. You might also try switching the plug wires around to see if you may have a couple of them bad. It would show up as 2 diff cylinder misfiring.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Mach10||11-04-2001||07:03 PM||||Roger: I've seen it before  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Two of them burnt clean off. No wonder the jag was running so rough  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>But yes, it's unlikely...<P>Sounds more and more like you might just have a couple of stuck injectors, if it isn't anything internal. Try tossing a bottle of injector cleaner (for what's it's worth :rolleyes <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> in, and see if it'll free 'em up. Might also try a can of TB cleaner... Watchit, though... They do have a tendency to eat catalytic converters. But then, if you are running that rich, it's probably injured anyway  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||10
Z||000005||Juiced_V8||11-04-2001||08:40 PM||||i have also had this problem on my wifes regal it had a 3.1 .<BR>the problem was a bad ignition module it burned up 1\3 and thus since the mod controls 1 coil with 2 cyls on it it burned up 2-4 portion of the mod "or somthing like that" id check the module just my 2 sense-lol||||reg||10
Z||000006||fastback87||11-04-2001||09:27 PM||||an easy way to test and see if the cylinder is firing is just unhook those two cylinders and let the wires lay on top of the intake "red one with fiero engraved in it".  Plug a known good spark plug in each wire and start the car.  Get out and look at the plug.  If it sparks, then that is not your problem (probably injectors or something in fuel delivery), if it doesn't then you can bet it is your electrical system.  Then you can start checking stuff like distributor cap, coil and pickup coil.  I would have to doubt it is your ignition module and only two cylinders don't work, sounds more like broken pickups in the cap or something wrong with the coil or pickup coil.  I hope this helps.||||reg||3
Z||000007||Huck||11-04-2001||11:50 PM||||Thanks for the help guys.  I removed the cap and checked it out, and it was looking very clean and normal.  I used a spark tester light, and I have lots of spark getting to the cylinder.  I managed to find a compression tester as well, though the thread size was much too large for this application, and it being sunday evening, I had no where to go to try reducing the fitting...  I really cant wait to check the compression, just to ease my mind that its not a head gasket (though I have no white smoke, there's no water in the oil, and I removed the thermostat cap and it was not bubbling).  At this point, I have to wait till next weekend before I have a free moment to go back up country and diagnose the problem.<P>Am I better off praying for faulty injectors right now rather than the head gasket?  Whatever it is, I should really try to get my winter beater on the road ASAP... <P>As for frying my catalytic, that doesn't really bother me.  SUpposedly we're still 2 years away from mandatory emmisions testing!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Thank you for all the replies, I'll bring this topic back friday when I go back and get a chance to try some more of your helpful remedies.<P>Huck<BR>87 GT T-tops||||reg||1
